{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794598782,"nonce":"5rTIcrD4lQX04VOlpdOQw26xflY=","message":"Stream\u003cResult\u003cT, E\u003e\u003e now crosses as hxn.Stream\u003cT\u003e, where T is a supported stream value and E names a #[hxn::data] type implementing Display. Earlier Ok values are delivered normally. Taking the first Err closes the read side, releases later queued values outside the queue mutex, and refuses subsequent sends. Iteration throws E's generated exception; listen sends it to onError rather than onEnd. The error value/message are serialized on the taking thread through the existing typed-failure mechanism. A Sender::fail channel failure retains its status, message and payload. Successful completion and an Ok(None) item remain distinct from failure/end.\n\nAnyStream::next_result is a safe Rust helper over an erased Stream\u003cResult\u003cT, E\u003e\u003e. It type-checks the stored stream, leaves wrong-type/pending reads intact, closes before invoking the error converter, and wakes waiters after cancellation. Stream drop uses the same unlocked cleanup. No hxn-abi implementation, dependency, layout or runtime export changed, and hxn-future gains no unsafe.\n\nThe model records the carried result in the manifest and discovers error types used only by streams. Generated take/helper names include both the value and error kinds, with existing collision checks. Value conversion remains the ordinary stream transport, including implicit element exports for object batches. HashLink/hxcpp stream takes convert stored failures to typed exceptions after Rust returns; CPPIA uses the same host classes. Literal Future\u003cResult\u003c...\u003e\u003e and Signal\u003cResult\u003c...\u003e\u003e remain refused; async fn returning Result is the supported fallible-future form.\n\nCore checks cover ordered delivery, one-shot errors, pending/closed/wrong-type reads, preservation of channel failures, reentrant destruction and cancellation racing four producers, with every sent value released. Generator checks cover distinct error names, stream-only exceptions, manifest types, bytes/unit/objects/optional/shared/batch values, helper declarations, unsupported shapes and collisions. Runtime checks cover scalar/data/unit/bytes/object/optional/batch streams, terminal cleanup of queued objects, successful completion, and listen ordering. Ownership checks run before other tests leave values for collection. Pantry.take_many demonstrates an order that yields earlier items and then stops with PantryException. Both manifests, the golden output and the design/convention documents are current.\n\nValidation, with runtime tests under timeouts:\n- macOS arm64: workspace tests, strict Clippy, formatting and diff checks pass. All nine counter modes pass. CLI and examples pass on Ash hybrid/JIT/interpreter, hxcpp, CPPIA, Ash wasm and Emscripten; C imports pass in every supported mode, with native SQLite where supported. Both native fixtures pass three repetitions per Ash mode; generated hxcpp and CPPIA pass three times, and generated wasm/Emscripten pass two more times after initial passes.\n- Miri: the full future suite passes all 38 tests with no skips/filters, using the existing ignore-leaks flag for process-lifetime workers. All 35 ABI tests pass. The new producer/cancellation race passes Miri seeds 0 through 7. The full native future suite passes three repetitions on each machine.\n- Linux x86-64 on the NUC: workspace tests, strict Clippy, all native counter/CLI/example/import modes, generated stock HashLink repetitions and both fixtures' Ash-mode repetitions pass. Linux Miri passes all 10 stream tests, including the new cancellation/drop cases. Every tracked source file matches the final local SHA-256 snapshot, and Haxe/hxcpp configuration remains task-local.\n- Mobile: all examples pass twice each on the iPhone 15 iOS 17.5 simulator through iphonesim and the Pixel 9 Pro Android emulator through ADB, including pantry's typed stream. Both devices were shut down afterwards.\n\nThe final public-API documentation clarification changed no executable logic; workspace/lint checks and the 10 stream Miri tests on both machines were repeated afterwards.\n\nNot run: Windows (awaiting push/CI), Linux wasm/Emscripten (both paths ran on macOS), a full Linux future Miri suite, or physical mobile hardware. Optional SQLite is skipped on the NUC because sqlite3.h is absent and by the Emscripten import script.\n\nTyped stream support is uncommitted and not pushed. This broader generator issue remains open for its other gaps. The separate cooperative-Miri coverage issue was committed as 7d7dae2 and closed.","files":null}]}